B.VA={"Penicillin allergy is a contraindication - ask about prior reactions before prescribing.":0,"Blood-stained sputum needs separate urgent work-up rather than empirical antibiotics alone.":1,"A large daily volume of purulent sputum raises the possibility of bronchiectasis and should prompt referral rather than repeated antibiotic courses.":2,"Persistent symptoms with weight loss or night sweats need TB work-up before or alongside antibiotic treatment - a live consideration in Egypt.":3,"Avoid in penicillin allergy.":4,"Reduce dose in severe renal impairment.":5,"A rash occurs if amoxicillin is given in glandular fever.":6,"Red flag: features of a life-threatening deep space infection (diphtheria, peritonsillar/retropharyngeal/submandibular abscess) demand immediate intervention before routine antibiotic treatment.":7,"Red flag: confirmed peritonsillar abscess or other suppurative complication requires hospital-level management (drainage, IV antimicrobials), not outpatient oral antibiotics.":8,"NEVER UNDER ONE YEAR. The article puts the gate positively: honey eases how often and how badly a child coughs, may help adults too, and is an option from age 1 upwards. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221) Below the first birthday honey must not be given, because of the risk of infant botulism. This is an absolute age gate, and honey is in every Egyptian kitchen.":9,"MIND THE OTHER BOTTLE IN THE HOUSE. Many Egyptian cold preparations already contain paracetamol; a parent giving a combination syrup plus a paracetamol syrup can pass the daily maximum without knowing.":10,"REDUCE IT IN THE MALNOURISHED CHILD - in severe acute malnutrition give 10 mg/kg, and no more than 3 doses in 24 hours.":11,"AND IN DENGUE WITH WARNING SIGNS - for a child, 10 mg/kg given 3 to 4 times a day.":12,"LIVER - use paracetamol with caution in hepatic impairment.":13,"ANTIBIOTICS DO NOT WORK HERE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE MESSAGE - they do nothing for a common cold, yet they are handed out in something like 30% to 40% of these self-limiting infections, and that is a major driver of resistance. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961) The chest is no different: acute bronchitis is nearly always viral and is managed supportively, with neither antibiotics nor antivirals shown to help. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221)":14,"WHEN AN ANTIBIOTIC IS ACTUALLY INDICATED - group A beta-haemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis; severe acute otitis media; sinusitis running beyond 10 days; or sinus symptoms that worsen again after first improving. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961) Nothing else listed here is an antibiotic indication.":15,"NO COUGH SYRUP IS PRINTED HERE. Cough and cold preparations sold over the counter do not work in children under 12, and have killed children under 2 by overdose; decongestants and antihistamines are therefore not advised below 12. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221) The common-cold article sets its own floor lower still: these medicines should not be given at all below the age of 4, for safety and for lack of any benefit. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961)":16,"WHY THEY ARE DANGEROUS, NOT MERELY USELESS - below the age of 6, both prescribed and over-the-counter cough and cold medicines carry real risk, and an overdose of an antihistamine or a decongestant can kill. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961)":17,"CODEINE - do not use it in children. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961)":18,"DEXTROMETHORPHAN - THE DOCUMENTS DISAGREE, AND BOTH ARE ON THE CARD. The clinical articles find no proven benefit in children for either dextromethorphan or codeine as cough suppressants, whether alone or combined with guaifenesin, and no definite benefit in adults either. The Egyptian formulary allows the syrup from 6 years and contraindicates it below that age; the tablets are contraindicated below 12 years altogether. It is not offered as a row here because no opened document shows a benefit in a child.":19,"BENZONATATE - its safety and effectiveness are not properly established, and it is not advised below the age of 10. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961)":20,"RED FLAG - THE FEATURES THAT MEAN THIS IS NOT A CHEST COLD: abnormal observations, low oxygen, blue lips, a change in consciousness, vomiting, pneumonia that keeps coming back, a weakened immune system, trouble swallowing, and a child who has gone off food. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221)":21,"RED FLAG - INHALED FOREIGN BODY: a story of choking, in a child above all, should put inhalation of a foreign body on the list. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221) A cough that started in one second, in a well child, is a foreign body until a chest is imaged.":22,"RED FLAG - PNEUMONIA: where pneumonia is suspected the usual work-up is a chest X-ray and a full blood count, with CRP checked selectively - a CRP over 30 mg/L in a fitting clinical picture makes pneumonia more likely. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221) Otherwise these are clinical diagnoses needing no imaging or blood tests unless something warns of a complication - that covers acute bronchitis, most viral infections of the upper airway, acute rhinosinusitis and allergic rhinitis. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221)":23,"UNDER TWO WITH WHEEZE AND FAST BREATHING IS BRONCHIOLITIS, NOT THIS - bronchiolitis mostly strikes below age 2. It opens with upper-airway symptoms, then wheeze, crackles, fast breathing, indrawing, and sometimes fever. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221)":24,"A WET COUGH THAT WILL NOT STOP IS A DIFFERENT DIAGNOSIS - protracted bacterial bronchitis is a wet cough going on 4 weeks or more, with normal spirometry and a chest film that is normal apart from possible peribronchial cuffing, and it clears on 2 weeks of the right antibiotic. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221) That two-week course is a decision made after four weeks of cough, not at the first visit.":25,"IT DOES NOT TREAT THE COUGH. Paracetamol has no anti-inflammatory action. It is given for fever and discomfort, and a child who is comfortable without it does not need it.":26,"SAFE WHERE ASPIRIN IS NOT - paracetamol is the preferred choice for anyone allergic to aspirin, for those with a history of stomach trouble, in pregnancy and breastfeeding, and in children.":27,"RED FLAG - Some clinical findings (high fever, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, otitis, cervical adenopathy, wheezing, productive cough, or dyspnea) add complexity and warrant further investigation for complications or an alternative diagnosis.":28,"RED FLAG - Antibiotics ARE indicated for severe acute otitis media, sinusitis lasting more than 10 days, or sinus symptoms worsening after initial improvement - they are not routinely needed otherwise.":29}
B.yU=new A.bD(B.VA,["Penicillin allergy: contraindicated.","Blood-stained sputum: urgent work-up.","Large-volume purulent sputum: assess bronchiectasis; refer.","Persistent symptoms + weight loss/night sweats: TB work-up before/alongside antibiotics.","Penicillin allergy: avoid.","Severe renal impairment: reduce dose.","Glandular fever: rash risk.","Suspected diphtheria or deep-space abscess: immediate intervention.","Peritonsillar abscess/suppurative complication: hospital care; drainage/IV treatment.","Honey: never under 1 year (infant botulism risk). No quantity or schedule is specified by the source.","Check other cold preparations for paracetamol; avoid exceeding the total daily maximum.","Severe acute malnutrition: paracetamol 10 mg/kg per dose, no more than 3 doses/24 hours.","Dengue with warning signs: child paracetamol 10 mg/kg, 3-4 times daily.","Hepatic impairment: use paracetamol with caution.","Uncomplicated viral cold/acute bronchitis: no routine antibiotics or antivirals.","Assess for GAS pharyngitis, severe acute otitis media, or sinusitis lasting over 10 days/worsening after initial improvement; these need a different pathway.","Avoid OTC cough/cold preparations in children under 12; they have no proven benefit and overdoses can be fatal. Sources give different lower age cut-offs.","Under 6 years: cough/cold medicines carry risk; antihistamine/decongestant overdose can be fatal.","Codeine: do not use in children.","Dextromethorphan: no proven benefit in children. Formulary permits syrup from 6 years and contraindicates tablets below 12; clinical sources disagree. Not offered here.","Benzonatate: safety/effectiveness unestablished; not advised under 10 years.","Assess urgently: abnormal observations, hypoxia/blue lips, altered consciousness, vomiting, recurrent pneumonia, immunocompromise, dysphagia or poor intake.","Sudden cough/choking: urgently assess for an inhaled foreign body.","Suspected pneumonia: chest X-ray and full blood count; CRP selectively. CRP over 30 mg/L supports pneumonia in the right clinical context. Uncomplicated cold, acute bronchitis, acute rhinosinusitis or allergic rhinitis: no routine tests without complication signs.","Under 2 years + wheeze/crackles, tachypnoea or chest indrawing: assess bronchiolitis.","Wet cough for 4 weeks or more: assess protracted bacterial bronchitis; do not start its antibiotic course at the first acute-cough visit.","Paracetamol: fever/discomfort only; does not treat cough.","Aspirin allergy: paracetamol is a preferred option.","High fever, pharyngitis/tonsillitis, otitis, cervical adenopathy, wheeze, productive cough or dyspnoea: assess for complications or another diagnosis.","Severe acute otitis media, sinusitis over 10 days or worsening after initial improvement: assess for antibiotics. Otherwise no routine antibiotics."],t.li)
